url-sanitize: code is MIT (see LICENSE). Rule data from upstream lists keeps
its source license.

The native `url-sanitize` binary (crates.io, GitHub Releases, Homebrew, Scoop)
embeds the default catalog, built from:

- ClearURLs Rules, https://github.com/ClearURLs/Rules: LGPL-3.0-only
- Brave Debouncer list, https://github.com/brave/adblock-lists: MPL-2.0
- Firefox Query Stripping records, Mozilla Remote Settings: MPL-2.0

So the binary as distributed is MIT AND LGPL-3.0-only AND MPL-2.0. The license
texts are in LICENSES/. The embedded rule data is the catalog.json shipped
with the crate source; replacing it with your own catalog is supported by the
`url-sanitize-core` library.

The AdGuard URL Tracking filter (GPL-3.0, via @url-sanitize/adguard) is not
part of the default catalog or the binary.

